\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Literature context of the 5$\sigma $ trigger thresholds, in total power (\S  \ref  {sec:background}). \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}EIRP against distance. Blue: this survey, plotted as $P_{90}$, the power recovered in 90 per cent of injection trials, for the deepest window per star (90{} stars, 1.30--39.63\,pc; filled: Class\nobreakspace  {}A, fine-channel; open: Class\nobreakspace  {}B, coarse-channel); orange: \citet  {Enriquez2017}; aqua: \citet  {Margot2023}; violet: \citet  {Mason2024}; left-edge ticks: deepest per-target limits of \citet  {Price2020}. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux. Green dashed: the Arecibo 2.38-GHz EIRP benchmark, a power scale only (\S  \ref  {sec:benchmarks}). \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}Native channel width against observing frequency for the same windows and programmes: resolutions differ by ${\sim }10^{7}$, so the panels must be read together and a lower EIRP here does not imply greater sensitivity to a hertz-wide carrier. \textbf  {This is not a like-for-like sensitivity comparison}, and the ordinate says so. This survey is plotted at 90 per cent measured recovery; the literature points are each programme's own $5\sigma $ threshold statistic, and those differ in definition, in the channel width they are evaluated over, and in the completeness they correspond to. We know of no way to convert the published values to a common completeness, so the comparison should be read as approximate. A further $\times 1.41$ is available to this survey from the individual parallel hands and is not taken here.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:context}{{1}{4}{Literature context of the 5$\sigma $ trigger thresholds, in total power (\S \ref {sec:background}). \emph {(a)}~EIRP against distance. Blue: this survey, plotted as $P_{90}$, the power recovered in 90 per cent of injection trials, for the deepest window per star (\NStars {} stars, \DistMin --\DistMax \,pc; filled: Class~A, fine-channel; open: Class~B, coarse-channel); orange: \citet {Enriquez2017}; aqua: \citet {Margot2023}; violet: \citet {Mason2024}; left-edge ticks: deepest per-target limits of \citet {Price2020}. Grey dotted: EIRP $\propto d^{2}$ at constant received flux. Green dashed: the Arecibo \ArecGHz -GHz EIRP benchmark, a power scale only (\S \ref {sec:benchmarks}). \emph {(b)}~Native channel width against observing frequency for the same windows and programmes: resolutions differ by ${\sim }10^{7}$, so the panels must be read together and a lower EIRP here does not imply greater sensitivity to a hertz-wide carrier. \textbf {This is not a like-for-like sensitivity comparison}, and the ordinate says so. This survey is plotted at 90 per cent measured recovery; the literature points are each programme's own $5\sigma $ threshold statistic, and those differ in definition, in the channel width they are evaluated over, and in the completeness they correspond to. We know of no way to convert the published values to a common completeness, so the comparison should be read as approximate. A further $\times \PolGain $ is available to this survey from the individual parallel hands and is not taken here}{figure.1}{}}
