\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Visibility-domain test for emission at the stellar position, for all 13{} stage-1 windows. Every visibility in the event's own channel is phase-rotated to the star and averaged: emission centred there gives a positive real part and an imaginary part consistent with zero, while emission displaced from the star is suppressed in the real part and in general leaves a non-zero imaginary part. $N_{\rm  vis}$ is the number of visibilities averaged, $\sigma $ the standard error over them, and `ctrl' the largest real part returned by eight control positions on the annulus of \S  \ref  {sec:statistic}. The events attributed to carbon monoxide are the test's controls: 7{} are recovered at $10$--$59\sigma $, HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 shows the displaced-source signature its foreground identification predicts, and one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris block could only be tested at a substituted frequency. All 13{} execution blocks were recalibrated from the raw archive for this test.}}{19}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:visibility}{{9}{19}{Visibility-domain test for emission at the stellar position, for all \NVisTested {} stage-1 windows. Every visibility in the event's own channel is phase-rotated to the star and averaged: emission centred there gives a positive real part and an imaginary part consistent with zero, while emission displaced from the star is suppressed in the real part and in general leaves a non-zero imaginary part. $N_{\rm vis}$ is the number of visibilities averaged, $\sigma $ the standard error over them, and `ctrl' the largest real part returned by eight control positions on the annulus of \S \ref {sec:statistic}. The events attributed to carbon monoxide are the test's controls: \NVisAttRecov {} are recovered at $\VisAttReLo $--$\VisAttReHi \sigma $, HD~48370 shows the displaced-source signature its foreground identification predicts, and one $\beta $~Pictoris block could only be tested at a substituted frequency. All \NVisTested {} execution blocks were recalibrated from the raw archive for this test}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The drift-following, continuum-subtracted fit at the stellar position, for all 13{} stage-1 windows. For each integration the channel the carrier occupies at that moment is taken along its recovered drift rate, a local continuum estimated from reference channels either side of the track in the same integration is subtracted, and the visibilities are phase-rotated to the star; `ctrl' is the largest real part returned by eight positions on the control annulus or four off-event frequencies in the same window. This is the sensitive estimator: it recovers $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris in 6{} windows at $6.1$--$9.5\sigma $ and still reports HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 as displaced, while the largest $|{\rm  Re}/\sigma |$ among the unattributed events is 0.93. The one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris window marked unreachable has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves.}}{19}{table.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:visfit}{{10}{19}{The drift-following, continuum-subtracted fit at the stellar position, for all \NFitTested {} stage-1 windows. For each integration the channel the carrier occupies at that moment is taken along its recovered drift rate, a local continuum estimated from reference channels either side of the track in the same integration is subtracted, and the visibilities are phase-rotated to the star; `ctrl' is the largest real part returned by eight positions on the control annulus or four off-event frequencies in the same window. This is the sensitive estimator: it recovers $\beta $~Pictoris in \NFitAttRecov {} windows at $\FitAttReLo $--$\FitAttReHi \sigma $ and still reports HD~48370 as displaced, while the largest $|{\rm Re}/\sigma |$ among the unattributed events is \FitUnattMaxAbs . The one $\beta $~Pictoris window marked unreachable has a drift track wider than the continuum reference leaves}{table.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The 4{} stage-1 spatial outliers with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics; $|\dot  \nu |_{\rm  max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm  eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this window, with the largest statistic any of them returns at the stellar position. All four events have at least one, 23{} in total, and none recurs; CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713's is examined in detail in \S  \ref  {sec:cp72} because it is the deepest and the closest match in configuration. All four have also been tested in the visibility domain (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:visibility}). $^{a}$\nobreakspace  {}the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead.}}{19}{table.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:unattributed}{{11}{19}{The \NStageOneUnattrib {} stage-1 spatial outliers with no astrophysical attribution, one row each. $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position and `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics; $|\dot \nu |_{\rm max}$ is the widest drift the stack evaluated for that window; $P_{\rm eff}$ is the response- and smearing-corrected threshold power. `Repeat blocks' counts other execution blocks of the same star whose tuning covers this window, with the largest statistic any of them returns at the stellar position. All four events have at least one, \NUnattRepeatBlocks {} in total, and none recurs; CP$-$72~2713's is examined in detail in \S \ref {sec:cp72} because it is the deepest and the closest match in configuration. All four have also been tested in the visibility domain (Table~\ref {tab:visibility}). $^{a}$~the released crossing frequency is stored only where the pipeline recorded a peak; for these the window is given instead}{table.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The visibility-domain test on all 13{} stage-1 windows, one panel each. In units of the measurement uncertainty: the real part of the visibility phase-rotated to the stellar position (blue circle), which emission centred there makes positive and significant; its imaginary part (red square), which such emission leaves consistent with zero; and the real parts returned by eight control positions on the same annulus the image-plane screen uses (open circles). \emph  {Top row:} the 4{} unattributed events, on one shared scale. None reaches $3\sigma $; 61\nobreakspace  {}Vir does not beat its own controls; HD\nobreakspace  {}14055 has a $3.2\sigma $ imaginary part, which a source centred on the star would not produce, but at this significance the test does not localise the excess either way. \emph  {Lower rows:} the 9{} events attributed to carbon monoxide, which are the test's controls, each panel autoscaled because they reach $59\sigma $. 7{} are recovered at the stellar position; HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 has a large real part but an imaginary part $7.9\sigma $ from zero, the displaced-source signature its foreground identification predicts; and one $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris Band\nobreakspace  {}3 block returns nothing, the one event whose test frequency had to be substituted from its siblings.}}{20}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:unattribvis}{{7}{20}{The visibility-domain test on all \NVisTested {} stage-1 windows, one panel each. In units of the measurement uncertainty: the real part of the visibility phase-rotated to the stellar position (blue circle), which emission centred there makes positive and significant; its imaginary part (red square), which such emission leaves consistent with zero; and the real parts returned by eight control positions on the same annulus the image-plane screen uses (open circles). \emph {Top row:} the \NVisUnatt {} unattributed events, on one shared scale. None reaches $3\sigma $; 61~Vir does not beat its own controls; HD~14055 has a $\VisHdImSig \sigma $ imaginary part, which a source centred on the star would not produce, but at this significance the test does not localise the excess either way. \emph {Lower rows:} the \NVisAtt {} events attributed to carbon monoxide, which are the test's controls, each panel autoscaled because they reach $\VisAttReHi \sigma $. \NVisAttRecov {} are recovered at the stellar position; HD~48370 has a large real part but an imaginary part $\VisHdFortyIm \sigma $ from zero, the displaced-source signature its foreground identification predicts; and one $\beta $~Pictoris Band~3 block returns nothing, the one event whose test frequency had to be substituted from its siblings}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Every stage-1 spatial outlier of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm  win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair and the remaining columns describe its deepest window: $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's 512{} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest laboratory rest frequency of the masked species \emph  {in the topocentric frame}, as the released catalogue column stores it, signed so that a negative value places the observed channel below the rest frequency. The disposition rule is applied in the \emph  {stellar} frame, after the frame chain of \S  \ref  {sec:frames}, and the stellar-frame offsets are given in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:bpicaudit}: for $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris they are within 0.46\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of systemic while the topocentric column reads about $-28$, and the difference is the barycentric and systemic terms, not a discrepancy. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when the stellar-frame offset satisfies $|\Delta v|\leq 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise; the $4{}$ unattributed pairs are consistent with the 3.2{} expected by chance ($p=0.40$). The HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 row remains the limiting case, a near-tie of rank dispositioned by velocity coincidence with independently published emission.}}{20}{table.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:flagged}{{12}{20}{Every stage-1 spatial outlier of the completed sweep, one row per star--band pair, generated from the released catalogue. $N_{\rm win}$ is the number of flagged windows in that pair and the remaining columns describe its deepest window: $T_\star $ is the search statistic at the stellar position (Eq.~\ref {eq:tstar}), `ctrl' the largest of that window's \NCtrl {} control statistics, $\nu $ the crossing channel, and $\Delta v$ the offset of that channel from the nearest laboratory rest frequency of the masked species \emph {in the topocentric frame}, as the released catalogue column stores it, signed so that a negative value places the observed channel below the rest frequency. The disposition rule is applied in the \emph {stellar} frame, after the frame chain of \S \ref {sec:frames}, and the stellar-frame offsets are given in Table~\ref {tab:bpicaudit}: for $\beta $~Pictoris they are within \BpicMaxAbsStel \,km\,s$^{-1}$ of systemic while the topocentric column reads about $-28$, and the difference is the barycentric and systemic terms, not a discrepancy. A pair is dispositioned as circumstellar or foreground emission when the stellar-frame offset satisfies $|\Delta v|\leq \MaskVWidth $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and independent evidence exists, and as unattributed otherwise; the $\NStageOneUnattrib {}$ unattributed pairs are consistent with the \ExpStageOneAll {} expected by chance ($p=\PStageOneUnattrib $). The HD~48370 row remains the limiting case, a near-tie of rank dispositioned by velocity coincidence with independently published emission}{table.12}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The external calibration sample as searched at 2026 September 17. \emph  {(a)} what the frozen pipeline returned on 1322{} windows it had never seen. \emph  {(b)} the 4{} unattributed outliers, all of them from the survey sample --- an earlier version of this table assigned three of them to the calibration sample, which \texttt  {holdout\_assignment\_v381.json} contradicts --- each a single cell a few per cent above the largest of 512{} controls, and none recurring at its own tuning in a further block. The repeat row gives the largest statistic the further block returns anywhere on its own grid; the \emph  {pinned} value at the first block's own frequency and drift, which is the stronger test, is 3.60{} for CP$-$72\nobreakspace  {}2713 (\S  \ref  {sec:cp72}).}}{21}{table.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:heldout}{{13}{21}{The external calibration sample as searched at \CampAsOf . \emph {(a)} what the frozen pipeline returned on \CampWindows {} windows it had never seen. \emph {(b)} the \NStageOneUnattrib {} unattributed outliers, all of them from the survey sample --- an earlier version of this table assigned three of them to the calibration sample, which \texttt {holdout\_assignment\_v381.json} contradicts --- each a single cell a few per cent above the largest of \NCtrl {} controls, and none recurring at its own tuning in a further block. The repeat row gives the largest statistic the further block returns anywhere on its own grid; the \emph {pinned} value at the first block's own frequency and drift, which is the stronger test, is \CpRecT {} for CP$-$72~2713 (\S \ref {sec:cp72})}{table.13}{}}
