\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Systematic uncertainty budget on $P_{90}$. The first block are independent errors on the quoted number and combine in quadrature; the second are carried separately: the sub-channel phase is an unknown about the transmitter, the decorrelation term is a \emph  {one-sided bias} that makes $P_{90}$ optimistic and cannot make it pessimistic, and the window-to-window term is a transfer uncertainty for the windows the campaign did not inject into. Folding them into one total would double-count the per-window bracket the catalogue already carries.}}{26}{table.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:p90budget}{{15}{26}{Systematic uncertainty budget on $P_{90}$. The first block are independent errors on the quoted number and combine in quadrature; the second are carried separately: the sub-channel phase is an unknown about the transmitter, the decorrelation term is a \emph {one-sided bias} that makes $P_{90}$ optimistic and cannot make it pessimistic, and the window-to-window term is a transfer uncertainty for the windows the campaign did not inject into. Folding them into one total would double-count the per-window bracket the catalogue already carries}{table.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The duty-cycle selection function, measured. Recovery probability against transmitter power, in units of $P_{90}$, and duty cycle $f_{\rm  dwell}$, from 3\,888{} injection trials. The abscissa is in units of $P_{90}$, which is the pipeline's nominal $5\sigma $ trigger multiplied by $\times 2.29$ for the spectral response and intra-integration smearing and then by $\times 1.2$ from the injection campaign, so a value of 1 here is $\times 2.81$ the trigger power and not the trigger power itself. The quoted $P_{90}$ thresholds apply along the top of this plot. Recovery falls away below $f_{\rm  dwell}\simeq 0.3$ and a transmitter on a tenth of the time needs several times $P_{90}$ to reach the same recovery, so the EIRP limits in this paper constrain persistent and high-duty-cycle emission and say progressively less about brief or scanning beacons. The dwell injections are zero-drift, so this selection function is measured for a stationary carrier and transferred to the drifting primary experiment; the joint drifting-and-intermittent cell is the one cell of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:classcomp} that remains unmeasured.}}{26}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dwellsel}{{9}{26}{The duty-cycle selection function, measured. Recovery probability against transmitter power, in units of $P_{90}$, and duty cycle $f_{\rm dwell}$, from \NDwellTrials {} injection trials. The abscissa is in units of $P_{90}$, which is the pipeline's nominal $5\sigma $ trigger multiplied by $\times \HanFacMed $ for the spectral response and intra-integration smearing and then by $\times \PNinetyOverTrig $ from the injection campaign, so a value of 1 here is $\times \PNinetyInTrig $ the trigger power and not the trigger power itself. The quoted $P_{90}$ thresholds apply along the top of this plot. Recovery falls away below $f_{\rm dwell}\simeq 0.3$ and a transmitter on a tenth of the time needs several times $P_{90}$ to reach the same recovery, so the EIRP limits in this paper constrain persistent and high-duty-cycle emission and say progressively less about brief or scanning beacons. The dwell injections are zero-drift, so this selection function is measured for a stationary carrier and transferred to the drifting primary experiment; the joint drifting-and-intermittent cell is the one cell of Table~\ref {tab:classcomp} that remains unmeasured}{figure.9}{}}
