\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Two-dimensional sensitivity against window centre frequency for all 1655{} searched windows, symbol by channelisation class, the axis along which completeness differs (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:classcomp}). \emph  {Left axis}: the nominal trigger threshold $P_{\rm  trig}$, an internal detection statistic. \emph  {Right axis}: the effective threshold at the \emph  {median} response, $P_{\rm  eff}=2.29\,P_{\rm  trig}$, which is the power to compare with a transmitter (\S  \ref  {sec:method}); the released per-window ratio $P_{\rm  eff,total}/P_{\rm  trig}$ runs $1.33$--$3.98$, so read a single window's threshold from the catalogue and not from this axis. Red rings mark the 9{} windows whose intra-integration smearing correction exceeds 1 per cent. Grey lines mark the Bands 3, 6 and 7 concentrations; dotted, the Arecibo 2.38-GHz EIRP benchmark, a power scale only. Note the two decades of scatter at fixed frequency: there is no single survey sensitivity.}}{27}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sens2d}{{10}{27}{Two-dimensional sensitivity against window centre frequency for all \NWindows {} searched windows, symbol by channelisation class, the axis along which completeness differs (Table~\ref {tab:classcomp}). \emph {Left axis}: the nominal trigger threshold $P_{\rm trig}$, an internal detection statistic. \emph {Right axis}: the effective threshold at the \emph {median} response, $P_{\rm eff}=\HanFacMed \,P_{\rm trig}$, which is the power to compare with a transmitter (\S \ref {sec:method}); the released per-window ratio $P_{\rm eff,total}/P_{\rm trig}$ runs $\PeffRatioLo $--$\PeffRatioHi $, so read a single window's threshold from the catalogue and not from this axis. Red rings mark the \NSmearLo {} windows whose intra-integration smearing correction exceeds 1 per cent. Grey lines mark the Bands 3, 6 and 7 concentrations; dotted, the Arecibo \ArecGHz -GHz EIRP benchmark, a power scale only. Note the two decades of scatter at fixed frequency: there is no single survey sensitivity}{figure.10}{}}
