\newlabel{fig:completeness}{{11}{33}{Stratified injection recovery. Grey, each of the \StratWindows {} configurations separately; blue, the pooled curve over \StratTrials {} trials. Dotted lines mark the 50 and 90 per cent levels. The spread between grey curves is the completeness transfer, measured rather than assumed. Right, recovery against drift fraction of the searched ceiling at $5\sigma $ and $8\sigma $, the axis the single-configuration measurement used to carry alone}{figure.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces What the drift search does and does not reach, as a physical selection function. \emph  {Upper:} the drift ceiling of each of the 403{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to the line-of-sight acceleration it corresponds to, $|a| = c\,\dot  \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$, and not at constant drift rate. \emph  {Lower:} the same boundary in the units the search works in, where it is not flat: the same 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$ is 1.4\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 115\,GHz and 10.5\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 872\,GHz. Reference accelerations, all frequency-independent and so horizontal in the upper panel: Earth's rotation (0.034\,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$), which a terrestrial transmitter carries whether or not it intends to; the most demanding known planet of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$); and the next most demanding, GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$). TRAPPIST--1 b{} is the one case the grid does not contain, and it misses by 0.01{} per cent (\S  \ref  {sec:discussion}).}}{33}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{12}{33}{What the drift search does and does not reach, as a physical selection function. \emph {Upper:} the drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to the line-of-sight acceleration it corresponds to, $|a| = c\,\dot \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$, and not at constant drift rate. \emph {Lower:} the same boundary in the units the search works in, where it is not flat: the same \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$ is \AccelDriftLo \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo \,GHz and \AccelDriftHi \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi \,GHz. Reference accelerations, all frequency-independent and so horizontal in the upper panel: Earth's rotation (\AccelEarthRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit (\AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$), which a terrestrial transmitter carries whether or not it intends to; the most demanding known planet of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$); and the next most demanding, \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$). \AccelWorstName {} is the one case the grid does not contain, and it misses by \AccelShortPct {} per cent (\S \ref {sec:discussion})}{figure.12}{}}
